      "abstract": "This article critically examines\u00a0Nadeem Aslam\u2019s novel The Wasted Vigil (2008) from an ecocritical perspective, highlighting the connection between displacement, environmental destruction, and the struggle for existence in Afghanistan\u2019s war-torn landscape. While typical refugee stories delve into the themes of trauma, loss of identity and political unrest, this analysis sheds light on the fact that the novel also represents nature as a silent witness and co-victim of human catastrophes. This research has integrated the concepts of eco-trauma and material eco-criticism with deterritorialisation theory to reveal that orchards, rivers, mountains, and ruins serve as layered sites of memory, violence, and resilience. Aslam reshapes post 9/11 fiction by breaking free from a U.S. centred\u00a0perspective and weaving together the experiences of Afghans, Russians, and Americans in a shared environment of grief. The narrative underscores the parallel between women\u2019s struggle for survival amidst patriarchal oppression and Taliban extremism and the degradation of the natural world, presenting both as dispensable entities. The study suggests that to wade through these fractured territories, human agency is not the sole factor to be considered, as the analysis aims to discuss the role of Aslam\u2019s storytelling in expanding the discussion of displacement to also include the aspects of the environment. Future research could expand the scope by including multiple works from various authors, offering a more comprehensive understanding of the subject matter.\nKeywords:\nDeterritorialization, displacement, Ecocriticism, eco-trauma, palimpsestic history, refugee narratives, survival\nReferences\nAhmed, N., Jawaid, A., Javaid, H., Naqvi, S. M., & Sarfraz, S. (2019). Pronunciation challenges faced by undergraduate students of the Middle East. Language in India, 19(2).\nCavalcanti, S. (2025). Scattering journeys, regenerative environments. A material ecocritical reading of the diasporic storyworld in Shaun Tan\u2019s refugee graphic novel The Arrival. MEDIAZIONI, 46, A19-A46.\nFransen, S., Hunns, A., Jaber, T., & Janz, T. (2024). Climate risks for displaced populations: a scoping review and research agenda. Journal of Refugee Studies, feae074.\nHiraide, L. A. (2023). Climate refugees: A useful concept? Towards an alternative vocabulary of ecological displacement. Politics, 43(2), 267-282.\nHussein, S. A., & Ali, S. A. (2025). Memory, Trauma, and Compromised Identities in Nadeem Aslam's Post-9/11 novel The Wasted Vigil (2008). Journal of Language Studies, 9(1), 227-236.\nIppolito, F. (2024). Environmentally induced displacement: When (ecological) vulnerability turns into resilience (and asylum). International Journal of Law in Context, 20(1), 74-91.\nKaur, P., & Jawaid, A. (2019). A case study of sustainable livelihood strategy of women porters at Entikong\u2013Tebedu. Opci\u00f3n: Revista de Ciencias Humanas y Sociales, (21), 1140\u20131156.\nKaur, P., Binti Karim, H. K., Jawaid, A., Sangaran, S., Myint, K. T., Singh, E. R., & Rashid, M. (2022). Stings of pedophilia: Case studies from Malaysia. Webology, 19(2).\nKaur, P., Jawaid, A., Saimon, R., Myint, K. T., Singh, E. R., Hassdee, H. B., & Aisyah bt Samsudin, N. N. (2020). Technological evolution of streetwalker in Malaysia. Talent Development & Excellence, 12.\nKiran, S. (2018). Deterritorialization in the wasted vigil by Nadeem Aslam. Available at SSRN 3095847.\nMorrissey, J. (2012). Rethinking the debate on environmental refugees: from maximilists and minimalists\u00a0 to proponents and critics. Journal of Political Ecology, 19(1), 36-49.\nMughal, A. J. (1998). A framework for the improvement of TESOL curriculum in Lahore, Pakistan, based upon a comparative analysis of TESOL in Birmingham, UK (Doctoral dissertation, University of Wolverhampton).\nNasir, Z. (2015). Women at the Edge: Crimes of Power against Women in the Context of Nadeem Aslam\u2019s Novel,